#: ../../complex-hierarchies.rst:4
msgid "Diátaxis in complex hierarchies"
msgstr ""

#: ../../complex-hierarchies.rst:9
msgid "Structure of documentation content"
msgstr ""

#: ../../complex-hierarchies.rst:11
msgid "The application of Diátaxis to most documentation is fairly straightforward. The product that defines the domain of concern has clear boundaries, and it's possible to come up with an arrangement of documentation contents that looks - for example - like this:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../complex-hierarchies.rst:35
msgid "In each case, a landing page contains an overview of the contents within. The tutorial for example describes what the tutorial has to offer, providing context for it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../complex-hierarchies.rst:40
msgid "Adding a layer of hierarchy"
msgstr ""

#: ../../complex-hierarchies.rst:42
msgid "Even very large documentation sets can use this effectively, though after a while some grouping of content within sections might be wise. This can be done by adding another layer of hierarchy - for example to be able to address different installation options separately:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../complex-hierarchies.rst:74
msgid "Contents pages"
msgstr ""

#: ../../complex-hierarchies.rst:76
msgid "Contents pages - typically a home page and any landing pages - provide an overview of the material they encompass."
msgstr ""

#: ../../complex-hierarchies.rst:78
msgid "There is an art to creating a good contents page. The experience they give the users deserves careful consideration."
msgstr ""

#: ../../complex-hierarchies.rst:82
msgid "The problem of lists"
msgstr ""

#: ../../complex-hierarchies.rst:84
msgid "Lists longer than a few items are very hard for humans to read, unless they have an inherent mechanical order - numerical, or alphabetical. *Seven items seems to be a comfortable general limit.* If you find that you're looking at lists longer than that in your tables of contents, you probably need to find a way to break them up into small ones."
msgstr ""

#: ../../complex-hierarchies.rst:90
msgid "As always, what matters most is **the experience of the reader**. Diátaxis works because it fits user needs well - if your execution of Diátaxis leads you to formats that seem uncomfortable or ugly, then you need to use it differently."
msgstr ""

#: ../../complex-hierarchies.rst:96
msgid "Overviews and introductory text"
msgstr ""

#: ../../complex-hierarchies.rst:98
msgid "**The content of a landing page itself should read like an overview.**"
msgstr ""

#: ../../complex-hierarchies.rst:100
msgid "That is, it should not simply present lists of other content, it should introduce them. *Remember that you are always authoring for a human user, not fulfilling the demands of a scheme.*"
msgstr ""

#: ../../complex-hierarchies.rst:104
msgid "Headings and snippets of introductory text catch the eye and provide context; for example, a **how-to landing page**:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../complex-hierarchies.rst:138
msgid "Two-dimensional problems"
msgstr ""

#: ../../complex-hierarchies.rst:140
msgid "A more difficult problem is when the structure outlined by Diátaxis meets another structure - often, a structure of topic areas within the documentation, or when documentation encounters very different user-types."
msgstr ""

#: ../../complex-hierarchies.rst:144
msgid "For example we might have a product that is used on land, sea and air, and though the same product, is used quite differently in each case. And it could be that a user who uses it on land is very unlikely to use it at sea."
msgstr ""

#: ../../complex-hierarchies.rst:148
msgid "Or, the product documentation addresses the needs of:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../complex-hierarchies.rst:150
msgid "users"
msgstr ""

#: ../../complex-hierarchies.rst:151
msgid "developers who build other products around it"
msgstr ""

#: ../../complex-hierarchies.rst:152
msgid "the contributors who help maintain it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../complex-hierarchies.rst:154
msgid "The same product, but very different concerns."
msgstr ""

#: ../../complex-hierarchies.rst:156
msgid "A final example: a product that can be deployed on different public clouds, with each public cloud presenting quite different workflows, commands, APIs, GUIs, constraints and so on. Even though it's the same product, as far as the users in each case are concerned, what they need to know and do is very different - what they need is documentation not for *product*, but"
msgstr ""

#: ../../complex-hierarchies.rst:163
msgid "*product-on-public-cloud-one*"
msgstr ""

#: ../../complex-hierarchies.rst:164
msgid "*product-on-public-cloud-two*"
msgstr ""

#: ../../complex-hierarchies.rst:165
msgid "and so on..."
msgstr ""

#: ../../complex-hierarchies.rst:167
msgid "So, we *could* decide on an overall structure that does this:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../complex-hierarchies.rst:180
msgid "or maybe instead this:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../complex-hierarchies.rst:198
msgid "Which is better? There seems to be a lot of repetition in either cases. What about the material that can be shared between land, sea and air?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../complex-hierarchies.rst:203
msgid "What *is* the problem?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../complex-hierarchies.rst:205
msgid "Firstly, the problem is in no way limited to Diátaxis - there would be the difficulty of managing documentation in any case. However, Diátaxis certainly helps reveal the problem, as it does in many cases. It brings it into focus and demands that it be addressed."
msgstr ""

#: ../../complex-hierarchies.rst:210
msgid "Secondly, the question highlights a common misunderstanding. Diátaxis is not a scheme into which documentation must be placed - four boxes. It posits four different kinds of documentation, around which documentation should be structured, but this does not mean that there must be simply four divisions of documentation in the hierarchy, one for each of those categories."
msgstr ""

#: ../../complex-hierarchies.rst:218
msgid "Diátaxis as an approach"
msgstr ""

#: ../../complex-hierarchies.rst:220
msgid "Diátaxis can be neatly represented in a diagram - but it is not the *same* as that diagram."
msgstr ""

#: ../../complex-hierarchies.rst:223
msgid "It should be understood as an approach, a way of working with documentation, that identifies four different needs and uses them to author and structure documentation effectively."
msgstr ""

#: ../../complex-hierarchies.rst:227
msgid "This will *tend* towards a clear, explicit, structural division into the four categories - but that is a typical outcome of the good practice, not its end."
msgstr ""

#: ../../complex-hierarchies.rst:233
msgid "User-first thinking"
msgstr ""

#: ../../complex-hierarchies.rst:235
msgid "**Diátaxis is underpinned by attention to user needs**, and once again it's that concern that must direct us."
msgstr ""

#: ../../complex-hierarchies.rst:238
msgid "What we must document is the product *as it is for the user*, the product as it is in their hands and minds. (Sadly for the creators of products, how they conceive them is much less relevant.)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../complex-hierarchies.rst:242
msgid "Is the product on land, sea and air effectively three different products, perhaps for three different users?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../complex-hierarchies.rst:245
msgid "In that case, let that be the starting point for thinking about it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../complex-hierarchies.rst:247
msgid "If the documentation needs to meet the needs of users, developers and contributors, how do *they* see the product? Should we assume that a developer who incorporates it into other products will typically need a good understanding of how it's used, and that a contributor needs to know what a developer knows too?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../complex-hierarchies.rst:253
msgid "Then perhaps it makes sense to be freer with the structure, in some parts (say, the tutorial) allowing the developer-facing content to follow on from the user-facing material, while completely separating the contributors' how-to guides from both."
msgstr ""

#: ../../complex-hierarchies.rst:258
msgid "And so on. If the structure is not :ref:`the simple, uncomplicated structure we began with <basic-structure>`, that's not a problem - as long as there *is* arrangement according to Diátaxis principles, that documentation does not muddle up its different forms and purposes."
msgstr ""

#: ../../complex-hierarchies.rst:265
msgid "Let documentation be complex if necessary"
msgstr ""

#: ../../complex-hierarchies.rst:267
msgid "Documentation should be as complex as it needs to be. It will sometimes have complex structures."
msgstr ""

#: ../../complex-hierarchies.rst:270
msgid "But, even complex structures can be made straightforward to navigate as long as they are logical and incorporate patterns that fit the needs of users."
msgstr ""
